¶
'''trencherman''' (tren′chėr-man), ''n.'': pl. ''trenchermen'' (-men). 1. An eater: with a qualifying word noting the degree of appetite: as, a poor ''trencherman''.

{{smaller block|You had musty victual, and he hath holp to eat it; he is a very valiant ''trencher-man''. ''Shak.'', Much Ado, i. 1. 51.}}

2†. A cook. ''Johnson.''

{{smaller block|Palladius assuring him that hee had already been more fed by his discourses than he could bee by the skilfullest ''trenchermen'' of Media. {{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Arcadia, i.}}}}

3. A table-companion; a trencher-mate.

{{smaller block|Mr. Wagg, the celebrated wit, and a led-captain and ''trencher-man'' of my Lord Steyne. {{right|''Thackeray'', Vanity Fair, li.}}}}

'''trencher-mate''' (tren′chėr-māt), ''n.'' A table-companion; a guest at dinner or other meal.

{{smaller block|These ''trencher-mates'' . . . frame to themselves a way more pleasant. {{right|''Hooker'', Eccles. Polity, v. 2.}}}}

'''trencher-plate''' (tren′chėr-plāt), ''n.'' In ''ceram.'', an earthenware plate of a special pattern, very flat and having a small rim, made by different potters of the eighteenth century. ''Jewitt'', II. 350.

'''trenchmore†''' (trench′mōr), ''n.'' [Prob. < OF. *''trenche-more'', *''tranchemore'', a fanciful name, alluding to the rough swashing manner of the dancers, < ''trencher'', cut, + ''More'', a Moor (cf. ''morris-dance''); cf. OF. ''tranchemontaigne'', a swash-mountain, a swash-buckler, lit. ‘cut-

readings.c2s1:
¶
{{smaller block|This Caravan . . . durst not by themselves venture over the main Desarts: which all this while we had ''trented'' along, and now were to passe thorow.
{{right|''Sandys'', Travailes, p. 107.}}}}

3. To have a general course or direction; stretch or incline; run: as, the American coast ''trends'' southwest from Nova Scotia to Florida.

{{smaller block|Vnder the name of India, heere we comprehend all that Tract betweene Indus and the Persian Empire on the West, vnto China Eastward, as it ''trendeth'' betwixt the Tartarian and the Indian Seas. {{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 452.}}}}

{{smaller block|Where the river ''trends'' westward into the main he set up a memorial cross. {{right|''Bancroft'', Hist. U. S., I. 91.}}}}

4. Figuratively, to have a general tendency or proclivity; incline; lean; turn. See ''trend''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'', 2.

{{smaller block|The discussion with his philosophic Egeria now ''trended'' away from theology in the direction of politics, or, as we now say, sociology. {{right|''E. Dowden'', Shelley, I. 164.}}}}

5. In ''geol.'' and ''mining'', same as ''strike'', 5.

'''II.''' ''trans.'' 1. To cause to turn or roll. [Rare or obsolete.]

{{smaller block|Lat him rollen and ''trenden'' withinne hymself the lyht of his inward syhte. {{right|''Chaucer'', Boëthius, iii. meter 11.}}}}

{{smaller block|Not farre beneath i' th' valley as she ''trends''
Her silver streame.
{{right|''W. Browne'', Britannia's Pastorals, ii. 3. (''Nares.'')}}}}

2†. To follow the course or direction of; coast along.

{{smaller block|We ''trended'' the said land about 9. or 10. leagues, hoping to finde some good harborough.
{{right|''Hakluyt's Voyages'', III. 206.}}}}

'''trend'''<sup>1</sup> (trend), ''n.'' [< ''trend''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] 1. A general course or direction; inclination of the course of something toward a particular line or point.

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|All
The ''trend'' of the coast lay hard and black.
{{right|''Whittier'', Tent on the Beach.}}}}

{{smaller block|Owing to the westerly ''trend'' of the valley and its vast depth, there is a great difference between the climates of the north and south sides. {{right|''The Century'', XL. 497.}}}}

2. A general tendency or proclivity; a final drift or bent; an ultimate inclination.

{{smaller block|What can support the dogma against the ''trend'' of Scripture? {{right|''Bibliotheca Sacra'', XLIII. 571.}}}}

{{smaller block|I have quoted these few examples to show the ''trend'' of opinion in respect to certain forms of atrophy.
{{right|''Alien. and Neurol.'', XI. 308.}}}}

3. ''Naut.'', the thickening of an anchor-shank as it approaches the arms.—4. A current or stream. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]

'''trend'''<sup>2</sup> (trend), ''v. t.'' [Perhaps for ''tren'', separate: see ''tren''<sup>1</sup>.] To cleanse, as wool. Also ''trent.'' [Local, Eng.]

'''trend'''<sup>2</sup> (trend), ''n.'' [See ''trend''<sup>2</sup>, ''v.''] Clean or cleansed wool. [Local, Eng.]

'''trender''' (tren′dėr), ''n.'' [< ''trend''<sup>2</sup> + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One whose business is to free wool from its filth. [Local, Eng.]

'''trendle''' (tren′dl), ''n.'' [< ME. ''trendel'', ''trendil'', ''trendyl'', ''trendull'', ''trindel'', < AS. ''trendel'', ''trændel'', ''tryndel'' (= MLG. ''trendel'', ''trindel'' = MHG. ''trendel''), a roller, roll, wheel, < *''trendan'', roll: see ''trend''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.'', ''trendle'', ''v.'' The noun also appears in the variant forms ''trindle'' and ''trundle'', q. v.] 1. That which turns or rolls, as a ball, a wheel, or the like; a roller; a trundle.

{{smaller block|Hir Ene as a ''trendull'' turned full rounde,
ffirst on hir fader, for feare that she hade,}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|And sethyn on that semely with a sad wille.
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 453.}}}}

{{smaller block|And Y schall cumpas as a round ''trendil'' in thi cumpasse.
{{right|''Wyclif'', Isa. xxix. 3.}}}}

2. A brewers' cooler. [Prov. Eng.]—3. The turning-beam of a spindle. ''Halliwell.''

'''trendle†''' (tren′dl), ''v.'' [< ME. ''trendlen, trendilen, trindlen'', < AS. *''trendlian'' (in comp. ''ā-trendlian''), ''tryndylian'' (in pp. ''tryndyled'') (= MHG. ''trendelen, trindelen, trendeln''), roll, turn; freq. of ''trend''<sup>1</sup>, or from the noun ''trendle.'' The verb also appears in the variant forms ''trindle, trundle'', q. v.] I. ''intrans.'' 1. To revolve upon an axis; turn round.

{{smaller block|A thynge that ''trenlyth'' rounde abowte chaungyth not place towchynge al the hole, but . . . towchynge partyes therof y<sup>t</sup> ''trenlyth'' rounde abowte.
{{right|''Bartholomæus Anglicus'', De Proprietatibus Rerum}}
{{right|[(trans., ed. Wynkyn de Worde, 1494), ix.}}}}

2. To roll along; trundle; bowl.

{{smaller block|The hedde ''trendild'' on the borde.
{{right|''Guy of Warwick'', ed. Zupitza (E. E. T. S.), l. 3712.}}}}

{{smaller block|A tickell treasure, like a ''trendlynge'' ball.
{{right|''Gascoigne'', Fruits of War.}}}}

II. ''trans.'' To roll.

{{smaller block|Y sawȝ a sweuen, and it seemed to me as a loof of barlich maad undir asshen to be ''trendlid'' and into the tentis of Madyan to goo doun.
{{right|''Wyclif'', Judges vii. 13.}}}}

'''trendled†''', ''a.'' [ME. ''trendled'', < AS. *''trendeled, tryndyled''; as ''trendle'' + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] Rounded like a wheel. ''Rel. Antiq.'', I. 225.

'''trenket†''', ''n.'' An old spelling of ''trinket''<sup>1</sup>.

